# Cloud Computing: Renting Computers by the Minute

> The cloud is other people's computers, rented by the minute. Behind
> that simple idea hides the biggest shift in computing economics
> since the PC: capital expense becomes operating expense, capacity
> becomes elastic, and a student's side project runs on the same
> infrastructure as a global corporation.

## 1. What "Cloud" Means

[definition]
**Cloud computing** is the on-demand delivery of computing resources
— servers, storage, databases, networking, software — over the
internet with pay-as-you-go pricing, provisioned in minutes and
released just as fast. The provider owns and operates the hardware;
the consumer rents exactly what is needed, when it is needed.
[/definition]

The defining characteristics (NIST's five):

1. **On-demand self-service** — provision resources without human
   interaction with the provider.
2. **Broad network access** — available from any device over standard
   networks.
3. **Resource pooling** — multi-tenant hardware shared among
   customers.
4. **Rapid elasticity** — scale up under load, scale down after; pay
   only for what is used.
5. **Measured service** — usage is metered and billed like
   electricity.

## 2. The Service Models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS

| Model | You manage | Provider manages | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **IaaS** | OS, runtime, data, apps | data centre, servers, virtualisation, networking | EC2, Compute Engine |
| **PaaS** | data, apps | everything up to the platform/runtime | App Engine, Heroku, Vercel |
| **SaaS** | nothing but your data and settings | the entire application | Gmail, Salesforce, Office 365 |

[important]
A reliable exam mnemonic — the **pizza analogy**: IaaS is a rented
kitchen where you cook; PaaS is a pizza delivery of dough and toppings
you assemble and bake; SaaS is eating at the restaurant. The deeper
the model, the less you control and the less you maintain. Control
and convenience trade off along the same line.
[/important]

**Serverless/FaaS** extends the ladder: functions (AWS Lambda) run
only when invoked, scale automatically, and are billed per
millisecond of execution — the provider even manages the container
your code runs in. "Serverless" still has servers; you just never see
them.

## 3. Deployment Models

- **Public cloud** — shared infrastructure operated by a provider
  (AWS, Azure, GCP) for anyone who pays.
- **Private cloud** — dedicated infrastructure for one organisation,
  on-premise or hosted; control and compliance at higher cost.
- **Hybrid cloud** — both, connected: bursts of public capacity on
  top of a private base; sensitive data stays private, spiky workloads
  overflow.
- **Community cloud** — shared by organisations with common
  requirements (government agencies, research consortia).

## 4. Virtualisation and Containers

Virtual machines (hypervisors like KVM, Hyper-V, VMware) virtualise
hardware: each VM carries a full guest OS — heavy but strongly
isolated. **Containers** (Docker, containerd) virtualise only the
userland above a shared kernel: an app and its dependencies are
packaged into a lightweight image that runs identically on a laptop
and in production.

| | VM | Container |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Isolation | strong (separate kernel) | process-level |
| Size | gigabytes | megabytes |
| Boot | minutes | seconds |
| Density | few per host | hundreds per host |

Kubernetes orchestrates containers at scale: scheduling,
auto-restart, scaling and rolling updates across fleets of machines.

## 5. Economics and Scalability

CapEx (buying servers for peak load that idles 90% of the time)
becomes OpEx (paying for the minutes actually used). Three scaling
directions:

- **Vertical (scale up):** a bigger machine — simple, capped by
  hardware, requires downtime.
- **Horizontal (scale out):** more machines behind a load balancer —
  the cloud-native way.
- **Automatic:** rules or ML add/remove instances as load changes.

Trade-offs to respect: cloud bills demand monitoring (a forgotten
load-test cluster can cost thousands), data egress is deliberately
pricey (lock-in pressure), and long-run steady workloads are
sometimes cheaper on reserved instances or on-premise — the FinOps
discipline exists to optimise exactly this.

## 6. Architectural Patterns the Cloud Enables

- **Load balancing** across zones for availability and throughput.
- **Auto-scaling groups** matching capacity to demand curves.
- **Object storage** (S3-style) for durable, versioned blobs.
- **CDNs** caching content at the network edge close to users.
- **Managed databases** with automated backup, patching and
  failover.
- **Regions and availability zones** — geographic separation for
  disaster tolerance; multi-zone designs survive a data-centre
  failure, multi-region designs survive a city.

Design for failure is the cloud's core discipline: any instance can
vanish at any moment, so systems are built stateless, with health
checks and redundancy replacing single machines that "shouldn't"
crash.

## 7. Security, Privacy and Lock-in

Data lives on shared hardware in distant jurisdictions: encryption
(at rest and in transit), identity and access management (IAM),
least-privilege policies and compliance certifications (ISO 27001,
SOC 2, GDPR data residency) dominate procurement conversations.
Vendor lock-in — proprietary managed services that resist migration —
is countered by containerisation, open standards and
multi-provider designs, at the price of some convenience.

## 8. Summary

Cloud computing converts computing from a product you buy into a
utility you rent. Its service ladder (IaaS → PaaS → SaaS →
serverless) trades control for convenience; its deployment models
(public, private, hybrid) trade cost for compliance; its elasticity
and managed services let small teams build planet-scale systems.
Understanding the trade-offs — not the brand names — is what the
exams, and the industry, actually test.
